<article class="env-example" id="00VW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00VW">Example <span data-tag="00VW">3.2.2.12</span>.</a> In the special case $n=1$, we can rewrite condition $(b)$ of Theorem <a data-tag="00VU" href="/tag/00VU">3.2.2.10</a> as follows: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
Let $f$, $g$, and $h$ be edges of $X$ which begin and end at the vertex $x$. Then the equality $[h] = [g] [f]$ holds (in the fundamental group $\pi _{1}(X,x)$) if and only if there exists a $2$-simplex $\sigma $ of $X$ which witnesses $h$ as a composition of $f$ and $g$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0042" href="/tag/0042">1.4.4.1</a>), as indicated in the diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  x \ar [dr]^{g} &amp;  \\ x \ar [ur]^{f} \ar [rr]^{h} &amp;  &amp;  x. } \]
</div>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> It follows that the fundamental group $\pi _{1}(X,x)$ can be identified with the automorphism group of $x$ as an object of the fundamental groupoid $\pi _{\leq 1}(X) = \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{X}$. </p>
</article>
